## Proof

Define
$$I(a) = \int_0^{\infty} \frac{1-e^{-ax}}{x\,e^x}\,dx, \qquad a > -1.$$

**Step 1. Well-definedness.** Near $x=0$, since $1-e^{-ax} = ax + O(x^2)$, the integrand satisfies
$$\frac{1-e^{-ax}}{x\,e^x} \sim a \cdot e^{0} = a,$$
so there is no singularity at the origin. At infinity, the integrand is $O(e^{-(a+1)x}/x)$; because $a+1>0$, it decays exponentially. Hence $I(a)$ converges for every $a>-1$.

**Step 2. Differentiation under the integral sign.** For $a>-1$,
$$\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\!\left(\frac{1-e^{-ax}}{x\,e^x}\right) = \frac{x\,e^{-ax}}{x\,e^x} = e^{-(a+1)x}.$$
On any compact interval $a\in[\alpha,\beta]\subset(-1,\infty)$ we have $a+1\ge \alpha+1>0$, so
$$\int_0^{\infty} e^{-(a+1)x}\,dx \le \int_0^{\infty} e^{-(\alpha+1)x}\,dx = \frac{1}{\alpha+1}<\infty,$$
giving a uniform dominating bound. Thus differentiation under the integral is justified, and
$$I'(a) = \int_0^{\infty} e^{-(a+1)x}\,dx = \frac{1}{a+1}, \qquad a>-1.$$

**Step 3. Integrate the derivative.** Clearly $I(0)=0$ since the numerator vanishes identically. Therefore
$$I(a) - I(0) = \int_0^a I'(t)\,dt = \int_0^a \frac{dt}{t+1} = \log(a+1).$$

Hence, for every $a>-1$,
$$\boxed{\int_0^{\infty} \frac{1-e^{-ax}}{x\,e^x}\,dx = \log(a+1).}$$

